fix(wasm): stop linking the rasqal SPARQL engine into every module

Apply inside libreoffice/:   git apply -p1 B_drop-rasqal-sparql-wasm.patch
Then save as an engine patch (engine/patches/) so apply_patches.sh replays it.

SCOPE -- this is deliberately the SMALL cut, not the big one.
The RDF stack cannot be removed wholesale.  raptor and redland are on the
unconditional load path for ODT *and* DOCX/RTF, so cutting them would break
document metadata:
  sw/source/filter/xml/swxml.cxx                 -> loadMetadataFromStorage on
                                                    every ODT import
  sw/source/writerfilter/dmapper/DomainMapper.cxx-> same, UNGATED, in the
                                                    constructor: every DOCX and
                                                    every RTF import creates an
                                                    rdf::Repository
  sfx2/source/doc/DocumentMetadataAccess.cxx      -> manifest.rdf is parsed and
                                                    serialised with raptor
                                                    ("rdfxml"), not by hand
  sw/source/core/text/porlay.cxx, itrform2.cxx    -> bookmark / content-control
                                                    lookups, per text node
Those all use getStatements() (triple matching).  None of them queries.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY DEAD: the SPARQL engine.
XRepository::querySelect / queryConstruct / queryAsk in
unoxml/source/rdf/librdf_repository.cxx are the only SPARQL entry points in
the whole tree, and a tree-wide search finds NO production caller -- only
unoxml/qa/unit/rdftest.cxx, which is not built for WASM.  With
--disable-scripting / --disable-gui / --disable-extensions the theoretical
Basic and UI entry points are gone as well.

WHY A PATCH IS NEEDED AT ALL
There is no --disable-rasqal.  configure.ac's entire redland section is one
libo_CHECK_SYSTEM_MODULE (system vs bundled); upstream LibreOffice has no way
to turn RDF or SPARQL off.  And redland hard-wires the coupling:
redland/src/rdf_query.c librdf_init_query() does
    /* Always have rasqal implementation available */
    rc = librdf_query_rasqal_constructor(world);
That one call is what pulls rdf_query_rasqal.o into the link, and that object
is the only referrer of librasqal (MEASURED with llvm-nm: 48 of the 51 rasqal
symbols librdf needs; the other 3 are query-language / result-format
enumeration in rdf_query.c and rdf_query_results.c, stubbed here too).

Note this patch does NOT remove -lrasqal from RepositoryExternal.mk and does
NOT stop rasqal being built.  It does not need to: once nothing references a
rasqal symbol, static-archive semantics mean librasqal.a contributes zero
bytes.  Leaving the build and the link line alone keeps the change minimal and
keeps rasqal.h available for compilation.

MEASURED SAVING (llvm-nm reachability closure over the built archives +
llvm-size per member; brotli ratios measured on the shipped soffice.wasm
sections -- 0.2284 code, 0.2803 data):
    before: librdf 33 members + raptor2 44 + rasqal 73 = 530,034 code / 129,572 data
    after : librdf 32 members + raptor2 44             = 270,590 code /  55,542 data
    saving: 259,444 B code + 74,030 B data = 333,474 B raw  ->  ~78 KiB brotli
Note librasqal.a (595 KB on disk) is the LARGEST of the three RDF archives.

FAILURE MODE IF THIS IS WRONG
librdf_get_query_factory() finds no factory -> librdf_new_query() returns NULL
-> librdf_Repository::querySelect throws rdf::QueryException.  A clean,
reported error; never a crash, never a silently wrong result.  librdf_world_-
open() still succeeds (rdf_init.c only checks librdf_init_query's return code,
which is now 0).

REGRESSION TESTS THAT WOULD CATCH A MISTAKE
 1. ODT -> PDF on a document carrying metadata, then ODT -> ODT round-trip:
    manifest.rdf must survive.  This is the raptor path; if this patch ever
    over-reached into raptor, it breaks here.
 2. DOCX -> PDF and RTF -> PDF: DomainMapper's constructor builds an
    rdf::Repository on every import.  librdf_world_open() must still succeed.
 3. A DOCX containing bookmarks / content controls -> PDF: exercises
    porlay.cxx getRDFRepository()->getStatements() during layout.
 4. Existing tests/run_pipeline.sh text + structure validation for ODT/DOCX.
None of these issues a SPARQL query, which is precisely the point.

SIDE FINDING (not addressed here)
external/redland/redland/redland-emscripten.patch.0 exists in the tree but is
NOT listed in UnpackedTarball_redland.mk's add_patches -- it is untracked and
inert, applied by nothing.  Worth a look separately.

diff --git a/external/redland/redland/redland-drop-rasqal-wasm.patch.1 b/external/redland/redland/redland-drop-rasqal-wasm.patch.1
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/external/redland/redland/redland-drop-rasqal-wasm.patch.1
@@ -0,0 +1,182 @@
+WASM: do not link the rasqal SPARQL engine.
+
+redland's librdf_init_query() unconditionally registers the rasqal query
+factory ("Always have rasqal implementation available").  That single call is
+the only thing that drags rdf_query_rasqal.o into the link, and that object is
+in turn the only referrer of librasqal -- so with it gone the linker drops
+librasqal.a entirely.  Two more librdf objects touch rasqal, purely to
+enumerate query languages / result formats; they are stubbed here too.
+
+LibreOffice never issues a SPARQL query: XRepository::querySelect /
+queryConstruct / queryAsk (unoxml/source/rdf/librdf_repository.cxx) are the
+only SPARQL entry points in the tree and have no production caller -- only
+unoxml/qa/unit/rdftest.cxx, which is not built for WASM.
+
+raptor and redland itself are NOT touched and must not be: ODF and OOXML
+import genuinely need them.
+  - sfx2/source/doc/DocumentMetadataAccess.cxx reads and writes manifest.rdf
+    via importGraph/exportGraph -> librdf_new_parser("rdfxml"), i.e. raptor.
+  - sw/source/filter/xml/swxml.cxx calls loadMetadataFromStorage on every
+    ODT import; sw/source/writerfilter/dmapper/DomainMapper.cxx does the same,
+    ungated, in its constructor -- so every DOCX and RTF import builds an
+    rdf::Repository too.
+  - sw/source/core/text/porlay.cxx and itrform2.cxx read bookmark/content-
+    control metadata with getStatements(), not with a query.
+Cutting raptor/redland would break all of that.  Only the query engine goes.
+
+Failure mode if a SPARQL query is ever issued after this: librdf_get_query_-
+factory() finds no factory, librdf_new_query() returns NULL, and
+librdf_Repository::querySelect throws rdf::QueryException -- a clean, reported
+error, never a crash or a silently wrong answer.
+
+Saving, measured by llvm-nm reachability closure over the built archives plus
+llvm-size per member: 259,444 B code + 74,030 B data = 333,474 B of raw wasm
+per module, ~78 KiB after brotli.
+
+--- a/src/rdf_query.c
++++ b/src/rdf_query.c
+@@ -62,6 +62,37 @@
+ int
+ librdf_init_query(librdf_world *world) 
+ {
++#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
++  /* WASM: do not register the rasqal (SPARQL) query factory.
++   *
++   * This one call is the ONLY thing that pulls rdf_query_rasqal.o into the
++   * link, and rdf_query_rasqal.o is in turn the only object that references
++   * librasqal.  Removing it makes the whole SPARQL engine unreferenced, so
++   * the linker drops librasqal.a entirely from the static archive -- worth
++   * ~333 KB of raw wasm (~78 KiB brotli) per module.
++   *
++   * Why this is safe here: nothing in LibreOffice ever issues a SPARQL
++   * query.  XRepository::querySelect/queryConstruct/queryAsk
++   * (unoxml/source/rdf/librdf_repository.cxx) are the only SPARQL entry
++   * points in the tree and they have no production caller -- only
++   * unoxml/qa/unit/rdftest.cxx, which is not built for WASM.  Everything
++   * that ODF/OOXML import and export actually needs from redland is triple
++   * storage and RDF/XML parse+serialise (raptor), which are untouched:
++   *   - sfx2/source/doc/DocumentMetadataAccess.cxx reads/writes manifest.rdf
++   *     with importGraph/exportGraph -> librdf_new_parser("rdfxml") (raptor)
++   *   - it enumerates with getStatements() -> librdf_model_find_statements,
++   *     NOT with a query
++   *   - sw/source/core/text/porlay.cxx bookmark lookups use getStatements()
++   * So raptor and redland MUST stay; only the query engine goes.
++   *
++   * Failure mode if something ever does query: librdf_get_query_factory()
++   * finds no factory, librdf_new_query() returns NULL, and
++   * librdf_Repository::querySelect throws rdf::QueryException -- a clean,
++   * reported error, not a crash or silent wrong answer.
++   */
++  (void)world;
++  return 0;
++#else
+   int rc;
+   
+   /* Always have rasqal implementation available */
+@@ -77,6 +108,7 @@
+ #endif
+ 
+   return 0;
++#endif /* !__EMSCRIPTEN__ */
+ }
+ 
+ 
+@@ -90,7 +122,12 @@
+ void
+ librdf_finish_query(librdf_world *world) 
+ {
++#ifndef __EMSCRIPTEN__
++  /* WASM: the rasqal factory was never constructed (see librdf_init_query),
++   * so there is nothing to tear down and calling the destructor would
++   * re-introduce the librasqal dependency we just removed. */
+   librdf_query_rasqal_destructor(world);
++#endif
+   librdf_delete_query_factories(world);
+ }
+ 
+@@ -310,10 +347,19 @@
+ librdf_query_language_get_description(librdf_world* world,
+                                       unsigned int counter)
+ {
++#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
++  /* WASM: no query languages are registered, so the list is empty.  NULL is
++   * the documented "counter is out of range" answer and every caller in
++   * redland treats it as end-of-list. */
++  (void)world;
++  (void)counter;
++  return NULL;
++#else
+   librdf_world_open(world);
+ 
+   return rasqal_world_get_query_language_description(world->rasqal_world_ptr,
+                                                      counter);
++#endif
+ }
+ 
+ /**
+--- a/src/rdf_query_results.c
++++ b/src/rdf_query_results.c
+@@ -965,8 +965,18 @@
+                                    const char *name, librdf_uri* uri,
+                                    const char *mime_type)
+ {
++#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
++  /* WASM: librasqal is not linked (see librdf_init_query in rdf_query.c), so
++   * no query-results formatter exists.  0 == "no formatter", which is the
++   * documented answer and what every caller already handles. */
++  (void)world;
++  (void)name;
++  (void)uri;
++  (void)mime_type;
++  return 0;
++#else
+   int flags = RASQAL_QUERY_RESULTS_FORMAT_FLAG_READER;
+-  
++
+   librdf_world_open(world);
+ 
+   /* FIXME - this should use some kind of registration but for now
+@@ -975,6 +985,7 @@
+   return rasqal_query_results_formats_check(world->rasqal_world_ptr,
+                                             name, (raptor_uri*)uri, mime_type,
+                                             flags);
++#endif
+ }
+ 
+ 
+@@ -1007,12 +1018,19 @@
+ {
+   const raptor_syntax_description *desc;
+ 
++#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
++  /* WASM: librasqal is not linked -- the format list is empty. */
++  (void)world;
++  (void)counter;
++  desc = NULL;
++#else
+   librdf_world_open(world);
+ 
+   /* FIXME - this should use some kind of registration but for now
+    * it is safe to assume Rasqal does it all
+    */
+   desc = rasqal_world_get_query_results_format_description(world->rasqal_world_ptr, counter);
++#endif
+   if(!desc)
+     return -1;
+   
+@@ -1048,8 +1066,15 @@
+ librdf_query_results_formats_get_description(librdf_world* world, 
+                                              unsigned int counter)
+ {
++#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
++  /* WASM: librasqal is not linked -- the format list is empty. */
++  (void)world;
++  (void)counter;
++  return NULL;
++#else
+   librdf_world_open(world);
+ 
+   return rasqal_world_get_query_results_format_description(world->rasqal_world_ptr,
+                                                            counter);
++#endif
+ }
